In context, I generally refer to "Artifical Human Tactical/Strategical
Intelligence."  Which would be shades of both, I presume.

>The two are very different. In a game, AI is not all that hard to do. But 
>an artifically intelligent opponnent will go from point A to point B in 
>the shortest path every time which the human player will ambush. Making 
>it smarter means adding randoms to its actions, even to the point of 
>doing an occassional surprising stupid move. 

The 'obvious' solution is mimicry.  Get human players of the
appropriate strategy-level to play the game, and grill them on what
factors influenced specific decisions at specific times.  Then create
an algorithm to weight those decisions according to the factors such
that the "AI" makes similar decisions at those specific times.

The problem with that obvious solution is twofold:  mimicking
intuition and gambles, and the sheer scale of the data that may be
involved.

